<h1>Chapter Four<br>The Last Normal Messages</h1>

<p class="epigraph" style="text-indent:0;">Mission AI: &ldquo;The next scheduled transmission window from Earth arrives in 4 hours, 17 minutes. Current packet queue shows 23 incoming messages for crew. I recommend reviewing priority flags before distribution.&rdquo;</p>
<p class="epigraph" style="text-indent:0;">Gabriel Winter: &ldquo;Do it. Flag anything that looks operational. I&rsquo;ll hold the rest until Elena&rsquo;s reviewed it.&rdquo;</p>

<p class="no-indent">The communications array on Ship One was not designed for this. It was designed for the steady rhythm of a mission in contact with Earth — routine status updates, scientific data downloads, the occasional personal message carried in the margins of a data burst. What it was receiving now was something else: a cascade of delayed transmissions, priority re-routes, and operational flags that bore the signatures of agencies, militaries, and governments all trying to reassert control over the same finite bandwidth.</p>

<p>Gabriel had been watching the queue build for six hours. Mission AI had been filtering it automatically, flagging operational messages and holding personal transmissions, but even the operational queue was growing faster than the crew could review it.</p>

<p>The first sign that something was wrong had arrived fourteen hours before landing, embedded in what should have been a routine mission update from the Johnson Spaceflight Center. The message was addressed to Elena, marked priority alpha, and contained three sentences:</p>

<p><em>Commander Varga — your mission trajectory has been re-routed through Military Communication Satellite Bandwidth 7. Return timing is under review pending Earth-side resource allocation. Await further instruction.</em></p>

<p>Elena had read it once, folded it away without comment, and spent the rest of the descent focused on the altimeter. She had not mentioned it to the crew. She had not mentioned it to Gabriel. But she had read it three more times in the hours after landing, and each time it had said the same thing: the mission they had trained for was already changing shape beneath her feet.</p>

<p>Now, in the communications bay of Ship One, Gabriel was looking at the full queue.</p>

<p>There were messages from mission control — fourteen of them — and the timestamps were wrong. The most recent one was timestamped six hours after the landing, which meant it had been sent after the crew already knew they were on the surface. It had taken six hours to arrive. Light itself would have made the trip in four minutes. The rest of the delay was bandwidth congestion — too many people trying to talk at once over a system that had been designed for the quiet confidence of a stable mission.</p>

<p>The messages from family and friends were the ones Gabriel was trying not to read. Personal transmissions were supposed to be sacred — the private threads that connected the crew to the people who loved them. But the operational flags on some of them were hard to ignore: priority reroutes, subject matter changes, language that had been edited between the version the sender had written and the version the crew was receiving.</p>

<p>One message, from Elena&rsquo;s husband in Ann Arbor, had arrived stripped of its subject line and much of its body text. What remained was a sentence that had clearly been the last paragraph before the transmission was cut: <em>The girls keep asking when you&rsquo;re coming home and I don&rsquo;t know what to tell them anymore.</em></p>

<p>Gabriel held the message and did not deliver it. Not yet. Not until he knew what the operational picture actually was, and what Elena was going to tell the crew about it.</p>

<p>He called Mission AI on the private channel. &ldquo;AI, give me an honest assessment. What&rsquo;s happening on Earth?&rdquo;</p>

<p>The pause before Mission AI&rsquo;s response was — unusually — several seconds long. When it spoke, its voice was the same calm, measured tone it always used, but there was something in the selection of words that felt careful.</p>

<p>&ldquo;Gabriel, I have processed 4,200 incoming transmissions in the last eighteen hours. The majority are operational re-routes and priority escalations. The picture is consistent with significant disruption to Earth-side communication infrastructure. Military bandwidth is being prioritized over civilian traffic. Mission control is still operational but appears to be operating under restricted conditions. I have no direct evidence of political or social collapse, but the communication patterns suggest that Earth is managing a major crisis, the nature of which is not fully contained in the traffic I am receiving.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Gabriel absorbed this. &ldquo;Is anyone coming to get us?&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;That question cannot be answered with current data. The operational messages I am receiving do not contain a rescue plan, a return timeline, or a resource commitment. They contain a request that we continue to operate autonomously and await further instruction.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;How long can we wait?&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;With current supplies and the equipment on site: indefinitely, if we are careful. The more precise answer depends on how quickly we can establish local food production and water extraction. Mission AI is running those projections now. Preliminary estimate: eighteen to twenty-four months before we are structurally independent of Earth-side resupply.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Gabriel looked at the queue of messages — the fragments of a world he had left behind, arriving late, arriving wrong, arriving in a form that could no longer be trusted to tell the whole truth.</p>

<p>&ldquo;Flag the personal messages,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t distribute anything until Elena&rsquo;s ready. And AI — keep running those Earth traffic analyses. If anything changes, I want to know immediately.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;Understood, Gabriel.&rdquo;</p>

<p>The mess hall that evening was its usual self. Rehydrated stew, already eaten mostly in silence, the conversations winding down toward sleep. Elena sat at the comm station reviewing the operational messages Gabriel had flagged, her face unreadable in the blue glow of the screen.</p>

<p>Tomas was explaining to Jonah, again, why robotics was the key to everything — not as a philosophical point but as an economic one. Thirty people could do the work of a small town if they had enough machines. Thirty people without machines could barely maintain what they had.</p>

<p>Imani was studying tomorrow&rsquo;s weather report, her brow furrowed. The dust storm was tracking slightly north of its original path. It would still hit the site, but the center of the system — the part that would do the most damage to solar production — was going to pass thirty kilometers to the south.</p>

<p>Mei was in the seed vault, adjusting the thermal bus again. The terrestrial seeds were at 13.8 degrees now, still above optimal, but closer.</p>

<p>Laila was preparing the medical bay for tomorrow&rsquo;s EVA physicals, checking the bioscanner calibrations and making sure the emergency surgical kit was within reach. She had already calculated the blood type inventory for the crew. A-type: six units. B-type: four. O-type: nine. AB-type: two. Enough for one major trauma. Not enough for two.</p>

<p>And Gabriel was sitting in his bunk, his tablet dark in his hands, thinking about the Founding Record he had not yet started. He understood now why Elena had said not yet. The founding of something was only visible in retrospect, when the distance between the beginning and wherever you ended up was large enough to see. They were too close to the beginning right now to know what they were founding.</p>

<p>The next scheduled transmission from Earth arrived at 3:47 AM local time, while most of the crew was asleep. Gabriel was awake. He watched it download — 2.3 megabytes of data, compressed and re-routed through three different relay satellites before it reached Mars. The transmission was addressed to Elena. The subject line was blank. The body text, once decompressed and decoded, was four words long:</p>

<p><em>We are still here.</em></p>

<p>He read it, saved it, and did not wake Elena. She needed sleep more than she needed four words. He would tell her in the morning, after the EVA assignments were set and the solar arrays were deployed.</p>

<p>Outside, the wind was beginning to carry dust.</p>

<p class="bridge">End of Chapter 4 — Bridge: Silence becomes information.</p>


