<h1>Chapter Five<br>Two-Year Thinking</h1>

<p class="epigraph" style="text-indent:0;">Mission AI: &ldquo;I am detecting an anomaly in the mission timeline data. The projected return window has shifted from Sol 720 to Sol 1080 without an associated mission directive. Recommend clarification from Earth. I will continue to flag this in all future communications.&rdquo;</p>
<p class="epigraph" style="text-indent:0;">Elena Varga: &ldquo;Noted. Keep flagging it.&rdquo;</p>

<p class="no-indent">The mission timeline was stored in Mission AI&rsquo;s memory banks as a sequence of numbered sol events — Sol 0 for landing, Sol 720 for the scheduled departure window, Sol 730 for Earth return. The logic was simple and brutal: two years on Mars, then home. Everything from food packaging to suit rotation schedules had been built around that assumption. The return ship — the vessel that would carry thirty people back to orbit, where the homeward fleet would be waiting — was a separate spacecraft that would arrive at Mars orbit six months before the crew was scheduled to leave the surface.</p>

<p>Except the timeline had shifted. Arun had noticed it first, running the orbital mechanics models during the transit, when Mission AI had flagged a trajectory adjustment that did not match any directive in the mission plan. He had recalculated three times before he came to Elena with it.</p>

<p>&ldquo;The return window is off by three hundred and sixty sols,&rdquo; he said. They were sitting in the small conference space that served as Ship One&rsquo;s command briefing room. The door was closed. &ldquo;That&rsquo;s a year. Not three months. A year.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Elena looked at the numbers. She understood orbital mechanics well enough to follow his reasoning, even if she could not replicate the calculations from memory. The return window was not just a date. It was a specific alignment of Earth and Mars that occurred once every twenty-six months. Miss it, and you waited. And if you waited, you needed more supplies. More power. More everything.</p>

<p>&ldquo;Can we make a year?&rdquo; she asked.</p>

<p>Arun had been thinking about this for three days. &ldquo;Not with the original mission profile. The food is packed for a twenty-four-month surface stay, including the return transit. If we add twelve months to the surface stay, we need to either reduce caloric consumption by roughly eighteen percent or find additional food sources locally.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;And we can&rsquo;t reduce caloric consumption by eighteen percent and do the work we&rsquo;re here to do.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;No.&rdquo;</p>

<p>She studied the orbital diagram. The red line showing the scheduled return trajectory, the yellow line showing the revised trajectory, the distance between them measured in time — a year of breathing, eating, drinking, maintaining, and hoping on a planet that offered none of those things freely.</p>

<p>She thought about calling a crew meeting. She thought about the look on thirty faces when she told them the return date was a year later than expected. She thought about what that announcement would do to morale, to work pace, to the delicate social equilibrium of thirty people living in three connected ships on an alien plain.</p>

<p>She thought about it for a long time. Then she closed the file and went back to the EVA schedule.</p>

<p>Not yet. Not until she had numbers she could actually defend.</p>

<p>Three days later, she had the numbers. They were worse than she had expected.</p>

<p>The food shortage was real but manageable — Mei&rsquo;s greenhouse could supplement the packaged diet enough to close the gap, if the first crops grew on schedule. The power situation was the more pressing problem. The dust storm that was now coating every solar panel on the site had reduced power generation by 31 percent, which meant the batteries were running down faster than the charging cycle could replenish them. The nuclear reactor was providing a stable baseline, but baseline power and peak power were different things, and the colony needed peak power for the water extraction equipment, the atmospheric processors, and the greenhouse environmental controls — all of which were running simultaneously for the first time.</p>

<p>Arun&rsquo;s solution was to reduce the colony&rsquo;s operational footprint. Run the water extraction during daylight hours when the solar contribution was highest. Shut down the atmospheric processors during the early morning and late evening, when temperature differentials placed the heaviest load on the thermal management system. Stagger the greenhouse lighting cycles so that only half the growing area was under artificial light at any given time.</p>

<p>It was not elegant. It was not even safe, by the standards of the original mission plan. But it would keep the colony alive until the dust settled and the solar arrays could be cleaned.</p>

<p>&ldquo;It&rsquo;s the math,&rdquo; Arun said to Elena and Gabriel in the command briefing room. &ldquo;I can make it work for twelve months if — and this is a very large if — nothing catastrophic happens. If we have a major equipment failure, if someone gets injured and we need to run the medical bay at full capacity, if the greenhouse fails — any one of those things and the math breaks.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;And if the math breaks?&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;Then we start making harder choices.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Gabriel had been listening without speaking. He was thinking about what Mission AI had told him in the communications bay — the possibility that no one was coming at all, not in three hundred sixty sols, not ever. He had not shared that with Elena yet. He was not sure he should.</p>

<p>&ldquo;AI,&rdquo; he said, looking at the ceiling speaker. &ldquo;Realistically. What&rsquo;s the chance Earth sends a rescue mission if we&rsquo;re not able to get back on the original schedule?&rdquo;</p>

<p>Mission AI&rsquo;s pause was even longer this time. &ldquo;I cannot assign a probability to that question with any confidence. What I can say is that in the 4,200 transmissions I have processed in the last thirty days, I have found no reference to a rescue plan, a recovery mission, or an emergency evacuation protocol for the Ares-One crew. That does not mean such a plan does not exist. It means I have not seen it.&rdquo;</p>

<p>The room was quiet. The hum of the life support system was the loudest sound in it.</p>

<p>&ldquo;Okay,&rdquo; Elena said. She stood up, folded her tablet under her arm, and looked at Arun and Gabriel with the expression of someone who had just finished a long and difficult diagnosis. &ldquo;Here&rsquo;s what we do. We plan for eighteen months. We tell the crew the return window has shifted — not because I&rsquo;m hiding it, but because I now have numbers to explain it, and the numbers are manageable. We start acting like a colony, not a visiting team. And we build the kind of infrastructure that makes us independent of any schedule that isn&rsquo;t our own.&rdquo;</p>

<p>She paused at the door. &ldquo;And Gabriel — keep analyzing those transmissions. If that picture changes, I want to know before anyone else does.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;Understood,&rdquo; Gabriel said.</p>

<p>She left. Arun followed.</p>

<p>Gabriel sat alone in the briefing room for a moment, thinking about what Elena had just said. Acting like a colony. It was a phrase that shifted everything and nothing at the same time. A colony was not a visit. A colony was a commitment. A colony was a place you planned to stay in, and planned to stay in permanently, with the full knowledge of the people who sent you might never come back.</p>

<p>He opened a new file on his tablet and titled it: <em>Ares-One Founding Record — Day 14</em>.</p>

<p>He began to type.</p>

<p class="bridge">End of Chapter 5 — Bridge: They move from uncertainty to math.</p>


