<h1>Chapter Six<br>Return Is a Supply Chain</h1>

<p class="epigraph" style="text-indent:0;">Mission AI: &ldquo;I have completed a full inventory of the Ares-One cargo manifest cross-referenced against the current survival projections. The results indicate a 12 percent shortfall in packaged food calories over an eighteen-month surface stay. The shortfall increases to 31 percent if the stay extends beyond twenty-four months. Greenhouse production can close the gap at current growth projections, but the margin for error is thin.&rdquo;</p>
<p class="epigraph" style="text-indent:0;">Arun Patel: &ldquo;Can we reduce consumption without reducing work capacity?&rdquo;</p>
<p class="epigraph" style="text-indent:0;">Mission AI: &ldquo;Not without risk to mission critical functions.&rdquo;</p>

<p class="no-indent">Elena had planned to give the crew meeting a framing — some careful words about the mission evolving, about resilience, about the strength of the group. She had rehearsed them in her head during the two hours before the all-hands gathering in the Ship One mess hall. Then she walked in, looked at thirty faces that had been watching her all week with the particular alertness of people who sensed something shifting beneath them, and threw the prepared words away.</p>

<p>She told them the truth.</p>

<p>The return window had moved by a year. The food math was tighter than anyone wanted. The supply ship that was supposed to arrive eight months from now might be delayed by the same reallocation that had shifted their own schedule. And no one from Earth had said, in any transmission they had received, that a rescue was coming.</p>

<p>She delivered it without drama, without softening. She stood at the front of the mess hall with her hands at her sides and laid out the numbers the way Arun had laid them out for her: food, water, power, oxygen, and time. The math of staying alive.</p>

<p>When she finished, the room was quiet in a way that she recognized — not the stunned silence of people who had not been paying attention, but the focused silence of people who were recalculating.</p>

<p>It was Laila who spoke first. &ldquo;What changes right now?&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;Everything,&rdquo; Elena said. &ldquo;EVA hours. Food rations. Maintenance cycles. Power allocation. Which experiments we run and which we defer. We&rsquo;re going to need a new operational plan for every system on this site, and we&rsquo;re going to need it in the next seventy-two hours.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Tomas raised his hand from the back. &ldquo;Are we being told to expect to stay permanently?&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;We&rsquo;re being told to expect to stay until Earth can retrieve us. And right now, I don&rsquo;t have a date for that.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Mei was doing her own math in her head. The greenhouse could close the food gap — but only if the crops grew exactly as modeled, only if the water reclamation system operated at peak efficiency, and only if the power allocation for grow lights was not cut further. She raised her hand.</p>

<p>&ldquo;If I need to increase the growing area by twenty percent to meet the caloric targets, I need to know now. That means labor allocation, power priority, and space in the habitat module.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;You&rsquo;ll have it,&rdquo; Elena said.</p>

<p>Jonah spoke from the side. &ldquo;I can free up two robotics operators for greenhouse support, but that means slowing the habitat module deployment. We can&rsquo;t do both at full speed with current crew.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Elena nodded. She had known this was coming. Every resource on Mars was a zero-sum proposition — you could have the habitat or you could have the greenhouse, but the same people who built one dismantled the other, and every hour spent on infrastructure was an hour not spent on food.</p>

<p>After forty minutes, the questions began to slow. The anger — and there was anger, Laila noted, a current of it running beneath the surface in several of the questions — was still present, but it was finding its way into practical channels now. How should the EVA rotation change? Should the food ration increase or decrease? When should the second greenhouse module be deployed?</p>

<p>These were the questions of people who were planning to stay.</p>

<p>Elena closed the briefing by outlining the next steps: a series of working groups, one per major system, that would report back within seventy-two hours with revised operational plans. She asked for volunteers. Twenty-six people raised their hands before she had finished asking.</p>

<p>She smiled — just barely, just enough. &ldquo;Good,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;Let&rsquo;s get to work.&rdquo;</p>

<p>After the room emptied, Gabriel pulled Elena aside. &ldquo;You&rsquo;re going to need to tell them more than this,&rdquo; he said quietly. &ldquo;Not today. But soon. They&rsquo;re going to need to understand that the mission they&rsquo;ve trained for and the mission they&rsquo;re actually on are two different things.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;I know,&rdquo; Elena said. &ldquo;I&rsquo;m working on it.&rdquo;</p>

<p>She spent the rest of the evening in the cargo bay, walking the aisles between crates with her tablet, looking at the labels she had memorized on the transit. Everything here was packed for a two-year mission with a scheduled return. She stopped at a crate of personal effects — photographs, books, a child&rsquo;s drawing that someone had folded into an envelope and labeled with a name she did not recognize. Someone on this crew had brought a child&rsquo;s drawing to Mars, expecting to return home and put it back on a refrigerator. The thought hit her with a sudden, unexpected weight.</p>

<p>She closed the crate and kept walking.</p>

<p>The next morning, the power allocation meeting ran three hours over schedule. By the end of it, Arun had a new load management protocol that shifted power priorities fifteen times per day based on solar availability, equipment status, and crew activity. It was a spreadsheet that looked like a piece of modern art — color-coded, highly conditional, and completely dependent on Mission AI&rsquo;s real-time monitoring to execute.</p>

<p>&ldquo;It&rsquo;s not elegant,&rdquo; Arun told Elena afterward. &ldquo;But it keeps the air on and the lights burning.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;Is that enough?&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;For now,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;For now it is.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Outside, the dust continued to fall.</p>

<p class="bridge">End of Chapter 6 — Bridge: The colony is born the moment the truth is spoken aloud.</p>


