<h1>Chapter Seven<br>The Meeting No One Wanted</h1>

<p class="epigraph" style="text-indent:0;">Mission AI: &ldquo;Crew stress biomarkers elevated: cortisol up 40 percent, adrenaline up 22 percent. Social cohesion metrics showing increased factionalization since the all-hands meeting. I recommend structured dialogue facilitation and a review of leadership communication protocols.&rdquo;</p>
<p class="epigraph" style="text-indent:0;">Laila Okonkwo: &ldquo;Acknowledged. I&rsquo;ll prepare talking points for the ethics segment.&rdquo;</p>

<p class="no-indent">Laila Okonkwo stood near the mess hall&rsquo;s nutrient recycler, her elbows resting on the cool stainless-steel counter as she watched the steam curl from the rehydrated lentil stew. The scent was earthy, faintly metallic — a reminder of the water reclamation system&rsquo;s endless labor. Around her, the hubbub of conversation rose and fell like tide: jokes about the dust clinging to everyone&rsquo;s boots, debates over the optimal angle for the solar arrays, the occasional burst of laughter when someone recalled a ridiculous Earth-side meme that had made it through the last transmission burst.</p>

<p>She had been reviewing the latest medical telemetry when the announcement chimed over the habitat&rsquo;s internal comms: all-hands meeting, mandatory, in ten minutes. A flicker of apprehension tightened her chest — not fear, but the familiar surgeon&rsquo;s anticipation before a difficult diagnosis. She wiped her hands on her towel, took a breath, and joined the flow of crew members drifting toward the central corridor.</p>

<p>Inside the Ship One mess hall, the room was already filling. Chairs scraped, trays clattered, and the low murmur of conversation swelled. Elena Varga took her place at the front, her commander&rsquo;s posture rigid, her eyes scanning the crowd with a mixture of resolve and weariness. Laila slipped into a seat halfway back, her notebook open to a blank page, her pen ready.</p>

<p>Elena cleared her throat, and the room fell silent.</p>

<p>&ldquo;Thanks for coming,&rdquo; Elena began, her voice clear and carrying no hint of the turmoil she felt inside. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve asked you all here because we need to talk about where we stand, what we know, and what we might need to do differently moving forward.&rdquo;</p>

<p>She paused, letting the anticipation build, then delivered the news that had been building in her mind since Arun&rsquo;s power calculations, since Gabriel&rsquo;s intermittent Earth traffic reports, since her own realization in the cargo bay.</p>

<p>&ldquo;The return plan we signed up for — two years on Mars, then home — is looking increasingly unlikely. Not because of a lack of courage or effort on our part, but because the systems that make return possible — launch vehicles, fuel production, orbital assets — may not be available to us when we need them.&rdquo;</p>

<p>The silence that followed was total. Not the respectful quiet of a briefing room, but the held-breath silence of thirty people who had just heard something they had suspected but had not allowed themselves to believe.</p>

<p>Laila watched the room&rsquo;s reaction spread in a wave. Ferreira, the young engineer from S&atilde;o Paulo, went pale. Jonah Mercer set his jaw and stared at the table. Tomas Hale closed his eyes for a moment. Mei Navarro&rsquo;s hand went to her mouth, then dropped, her expression settling into something careful and controlled.</p>

<p>&ldquo;The timeline has shifted by at least a year,&rdquo; Elena continued. &ldquo;Possibly longer. We&rsquo;ve been managing our resources on the assumption of a two-year stay. We&rsquo;re now planning for eighteen months minimum, and we need to be prepared for longer. This means changes to how we work, how we eat, how we allocate power, and how we think about the future.&rdquo;</p>

<p>From the back, a voice — one of the junior crew members, not someone Laila immediately recognized — spoke with an edge that cut through the careful calm of the room.</p>

<p>&ldquo;So we&rsquo;re stuck here.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;We&rsquo;re not stuck,&rdquo; Elena said, her voice steady. &ldquo;We&rsquo;re building something. But we need to build it as though we&rsquo;re staying, not as though we&rsquo;re visiting.&rdquo;</p>

<p>The word landed in the room with a weight that Elena had not quite intended and could not have predicted. Colony. Not crew. Not team. Colony. A word that carried with it the implication of permanence, of settlement, of a future that was being made rather than returned to.</p>

<p>Gabriel was writing in his tablet, his fingers moving fast. He was thinking about how he would describe this moment — what words he would use when the Founding Record was read by someone, someday, who wanted to understand what it had felt like to be here at the beginning.</p>

<p>After forty minutes, the questions began to slow. The anger was still present, but it was finding its way into practical channels now. How should the EVA rotation change? Should the food ration increase or decrease? When should the second greenhouse module be deployed?</p>

<p>These were good questions. These were the questions of people who were planning to stay.</p>

<p>Elena closed the briefing by outlining the next steps: a series of working groups, one per major system, that would report back within seventy-two hours with revised operational plans. She asked for volunteers. Twenty-six people raised their hands before she had finished asking.</p>

<p>She smiled — just barely, just enough. &ldquo;Good,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;Let&rsquo;s get to work.&rdquo;</p>

<p>As the room emptied, Laila approached the front. &ldquo;You handled that well,&rdquo; she said quietly.</p>

<p>&ldquo;I told them the truth,&rdquo; Elena said. &ldquo;That&rsquo;s not handling it well. That&rsquo;s just doing the job.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Laila studied her commander&rsquo;s face for a moment. The truth was that Elena was exhausted — she had been running on four hours of sleep and six cups of rehydrated coffee for three days. But she was also clear. And the crew needed clear right now more than they needed comfortable.</p>

<p>&ldquo;Get some sleep tonight,&rdquo; Laila said. &ldquo;The working groups will be there tomorrow.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Elena nodded and walked back toward the comm station. Laila watched her go, then opened her tablet and began typing her own report: <em>All-hands stress assessment — elevated but contained. Recommend continued monitoring. No medical intervention required at this time.</em></p>

<p>She added one more line: <em>The colony is functioning. That is the most important medical fact of the day.</em></p>

<p>Mission AI&rsquo;s voice, transmitted privately to her tablet: &ldquo;I agree, Doctor. And I am monitoring all crew biomarkers continuously. I will alert you to any significant changes.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Outside, the wind had picked up again. The dust was still falling.</p>

<p class="bridge">End of Chapter 7 — Bridge: The first test of the new reality comes from the planet itself.</p>


