<h1>Chapter Eight<br>Dust Weather</h1>

<p class="epigraph" style="text-indent:0;">Mission AI: &ldquo;I am tracking a regional dust event currently centered 340 kilometers east-northeast of the Ares-One site. The system is broader than initially projected and is producing sustained winds of 60 to 80 kilometers per hour at surface level. Estimated time to site impact: 8 hours, 22 minutes. I recommend immediate commencement of solar panel protection protocols.&rdquo;</p>
<p class="epigraph" style="text-indent:0;">Imani Sato: &ldquo;Copy. Starting EVA deployment now.&rdquo;</p>

<p class="no-indent">Imani Sato had seen dust storms on Mars from orbit — the planet-wide events that could swallow continents and blot out the sun for weeks. This was not one of those. This was a regional storm, the kind that happened several times per Martian year, and the data Mission AI was feeding her said it was moving faster than the models predicted.</p>

<p>She pulled on her EVA suit in the Ship Two airlock, checking each seal twice the way she had been trained, the way she had trained herself to do on every EVA regardless of time pressure. The suit was number seven in the rotation — a newer model with updated dust filters that the engineers in Buenos Aires had spent three years refining after the previous Mars mission had documented the toll Martian regolith took on seal surfaces. She zipped the inner lock and activated the suit&rsquo;s environmental control.</p>

<p>&ldquo;Air&rsquo;s clean,&rdquo; she reported. &ldquo;Pressure nominal. Starting depress.&rdquo;</p>

<p>The airlock cycled. The outer door opened onto a landscape that had changed in eight hours. The sky, which had been a pale salmon color at dawn, was now the color of a dirty blanket — a thick, even brown that diffused the sunlight into a dim amber glow and turned the horizon into a smudge. The wind was blowing at a steady thirty kilometers per hour, carrying fine particles that ticked against her visor like rain made of powder.</p>

<p>This was the thing that people who had never been to Mars did not understand about dust storms: they were not like sandstorms on Earth, where you could see the grains and feel the impact. On Mars, the dust was fine enough to be almost invisible until you looked at the sky, and by then it was everywhere.</p>

<p>&ldquo;Starting panel cleaning protocol,&rdquo; she reported. &ldquo;AI, confirm robot assist status.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;Units R-7 through R-12 are deploying to assist,&rdquo; Mission AI replied. &ldquo;However, I am detecting a fault in Unit R-9&rsquo;s left manipulator arm. Diagnostic suggests contamination of the rotational joint — consistent with dust intrusion. I recommend removing R-9 from active service until the joint can be cleaned and relubricated.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Imani looked across the array toward where R-9 was trudging. Its left arm was moving slightly stiffly, the wrist joint catching on each sweep.</p>

<p>&ldquo;R-9, return to maintenance bay,&rdquo; she called over the robot&rsquo;s direct frequency.</p>

<p>R-9 stopped. Turned its head — a gesture that was unnecessary for the robot but had been programmed in for human psychological comfort. &ldquo;Understood, Imani. Returning.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Dust was the enemy of everything mechanical on Mars. It was fine enough to get past seals, abrasive enough to wear down moving parts, and reactive enough to interfere with electronics. Every machine on this site was in a constant low-grade battle with dust, and every dust storm accelerated that battle.</p>

<p>Behind her, in the habitat module, Arun was running the power numbers in real time. The solar contribution had dropped from a projected 89 kilowatts at this time of day to 61 kilowatts — a 31 percent reduction that was translating directly into a drain on the battery reserves. The nuclear reactor was picking up the slack, but the reactor&rsquo;s output was fixed, and the batteries could only be charged so fast.</p>

<p>&ldquo;Arun,&rdquo; Mission AI said in his earpiece, &ldquo;the battery bank is at 67 percent and falling. If the dust storm persists beyond eighteen hours, I recommend we begin selective load shedding on the atmospheric processors.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;Do it,&rdquo; Arun said. &ldquo;Keep the CO2 scrubbers on. We shed everything else we can.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;Confirming priority list. Life support, then communication, then scientific equipment, then habitat comfort systems.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;Correct.&rdquo;</p>

<p>He pulled up the load management spreadsheet — the color-coded monstrosity that he had built in three hours two days ago and had been refining every six hours since. The math was getting tighter. The storm had cost them 31 percent of solar generation. If it lasted more than a day, they would start drawing down reserves they could not easily replenish.</p>

<p>Jonah appeared at his shoulder. &ldquo;The habitat module inflation is on hold until the storm passes. We&rsquo;ve got thirty people in three ships that are now running at 85 percent capacity because we can&rsquo;t pressurize the new module. It&rsquo;s getting crowded.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;I know.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;The suit seals are starting to show dust degradation. I&rsquo;m seeing micro-abrasions on six units.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;Get them to maintenance. Rotate the clean ones forward.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Jonah hesitated. &ldquo;We don&rsquo;t have six clean ones if we&rsquo;re doing daily EVAs.&rdquo;</p>

<p>Arun looked at the load chart. &ldquo;Then we do four EVAs a day instead of six and rotate the suits more carefully. I want a full suit audit by tonight.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;Copy.&rdquo;</p>

<p>The storm continued. The dust continued to fall.</p>

<p>By midnight — Martian midnight, the arbitrary human construct that meant nothing to the planet outside — the visibility at the site had dropped to under three hundred meters. The solar panels were generating 44 kilowatts, barely a third of their rated capacity. The battery bank was at 41 percent. Mission AI had already shed the atmospheric humidifiers and the food storage refrigeration unit to save power. The crew was crowded into the pressurized volume of Ships One and Two, breathing air that was slightly too dry and eating rations that were slightly too warm.</p>

<p>Elena stood at the viewport of Ship One, looking out at the brown darkness. Nothing was visible beyond fifty meters. The wind had dropped to a steady twenty kilometers per hour, but the dust remained suspended — the particular cruelty of a Martian dust storm, which could coat every surface for days after the wind had stopped.</p>

<p>&ldquo;How long?&rdquo; she asked Mission AI.</p>

<p>&ldquo;The core of the storm is passing the site now. I estimate six to ten hours before visibility improves to acceptable levels. However, the dust that has already settled will require manual cleaning of all exposed surfaces. Solar panel efficiency will not return to full capacity until the panel array is cleared.&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;And the batteries?&rdquo;</p>

<p>&ldquo;Will recover within twenty-four hours of restored solar generation, assuming no further degradation events.&rdquo;</p>

<p>She nodded. She had been commander for twenty days. In twenty days, she had landed three ships, connected them into a single pressurized habitat, begun the construction of a permanent settlement, faced down an equipment failure that cost them critical spares, and waited out a dust storm that reduced their power generation by two-thirds and made thirty people sleep elbow-to-elbow in spaces designed for twelve.</p>

<p>She was not tired. She was something more precise than tired. She was awake in a way she had not known she could be.</p>

<p>Behind her, in the mess hall, Gabriel was recording the day&rsquo;s data in the Founding Record. He was not recording it as a crisis. He was recording it as Tuesday.</p>

<p class="bridge">End of Chapter 8 — Bridge: To live here, they need local water before their imported reserves become a countdown.</p>


